133.text:
 inputsFor selects which inputs purr builds from. A host then points at one of those
inputs with meta.roles.<role>. Modules always keep the original inputs argument.
The replacement only affects how purr builds pkgs, system configs, home configs, and
the merged lib. See Host Meta: roles for the role
reference. # flake.nix
{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-25.05";
    nixpkgs-unstable.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
    purr.url = "github:nixcafe/purr";
  };

  outputs = inputs:
    inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake {
      inherit inputs;
      src = ./.;
      inputsFor = { inputs, ... }: {
        inherit (inputs) nixpkgs;
        inherit (inputs) nixpkgs-unstable;
      };
      hosts.server.meta.roles.nixpkgs = "nixpkgs-unstable";
    };
} The same role can be set in meta.nix: # systems/x86_64-linux/server/meta.nix
{ roles.nixpkgs = "nixpkgs-unstable"; } Supported role keys are nixpkgs, home-manager, and nix-darwin. Homes inherit
their linked system's nixpkgs; unmatched homes use the effective-input default.

134.text:
 A per-system formatter is discovered from formatters/ or formatter/. The
default.nix receives { pkgs, lib, system, namespace, inputs, ... } and must return
a derivation: # formatters/default.nix
{ pkgs, ... }: pkgs.nixfmt-rfc-style This produces formatter.<system>, which nix fmt uses. outputsBuilder can override
it: inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake {
  inherit inputs;
  src = ./.;
  outputsBuilder = { pkgs, ... }: {
    formatter = pkgs.alejandra;
  };
}

135.text:
 Modules under packages/ are exposed as packages.<system>.<name>. When
packagesToPkgs = true (the default), they are also registered in pkgs, so any
per-system module or host config can reference them as pkgs.<name>: # packages/hello/default.nix
{ lib, ... }: { /* ... */ } # checks/lint/default.nix
{ pkgs, ... }: pkgs.hello   # the discovered package Each discovered package is wrapped like a callPackage package, so
.override and .overrideAttrs work, and packages can depend on each other: # packages/wrapper/default.nix
# `hello` resolves from `pkgs`
{ hello, ... }: hello.override { /* ... */ } Registration is a nixpkgs overlay built on the package-set fixpoint. One-way
dependencies resolve lazily. Mutually recursive packages recurse, as in any nixpkgs
overlay. Set packagesToPkgs = false to keep packages out of pkgs. They remain available as
packages.<system>.<name>: inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake {
  inherit inputs;
  src = ./.;
  packagesToPkgs = false;
}

136.text:
 legacyPackages.<system>.* is discovered from legacyPackages/. These packages are
not added to pkgs: # legacyPackages/hello/default.nix
{ pkgs, ... }: pkgs.hello inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake {
  inherit inputs;
  src = ./.;
  legacyPackagesByName = true;   # also scan legacyPackages/by-name/<shard>/<name>/package.nix
} Build one with nix build .#legacyPackages.<system>.hello.

138.text:
 apps/<name>/default.nix becomes apps.<system>.<name> and must return a flake app: # apps/hello/default.nix
{ pkgs, ... }: {
  type = "app";
  program = "${pkgs.hello}/bin/hello";
}

139.text:
 overlays/<name>/default.nix becomes overlays.<name>, and
templates/<name>/default.nix becomes templates.<name>: # overlays/custom/default.nix
_final: prev: {
  hello = prev.hello.overrideAttrs (_: { doCheck = false; });
}

# templates/rust/default.nix
{
  path = ./.;
  description = "A Rust project";
}

140.text:
 Pass custom values to every auto-discovered module: inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake {
  inherit inputs;
  src = ./.;
  extraArgs = {
    deploymentTarget = "production";
  };
} Modules receive extraArgs values as function parameters: # packages/myapp/default.nix
{ deploymentTarget, pkgs, ... }:
pkgs.writeText "myapp" "target: ${deploymentTarget}" The keys are passed to packages, shells, checks, apps, templates, system
specialArgs, home extraSpecialArgs, and outputsBuilder. purr's own keys
(inputs, pkgs, namespace, lib, and similar) override extraArgs on conflict.
